    "voice",
    "A closure is a function that keeps references to variables from the scope it was created in, so those variables stay alive as long as the function does. That's why closures hold memory: the captured variables aren't freed when the outer function returns, so if you keep a closure around, you keep its captured data around too."
   ]
  ]
 },
